</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Effect of Intra-Expert Compression</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Performance Comparisons ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Sub-MoE: Efficient Mixture-of-Expert LLMs Compression via Subspace Expert Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares Sub-MoE<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> against MC-SMoE under various intra-expert compression ratios. Our method consistently outperforms MC-SMoE across all settings, with dramatic differences at higher compression rates. When compressing Mixtral 4x7B with a 10% ratio, Sub-MoE<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> maintains reasonable perplexity scores (8.60, 54.27, 16.15 on WikiText-2, PTB, and C4), while MC-SMoE suffers catastrophic degradation. This performance gap widens as compression increases, demonstrating Sub-MoE<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>’s robustness to parameter reduction. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Sub-MoE: Efficient Mixture-of-Expert LLMs Compression via Subspace Expert Merging" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (Left), Sub-MoE<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> with fine-tuning (Sub-MoE<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>+FT) is able to additionally recover the accuracy significantly compared to more compressors, achieving gains of 4-6% over the base Sub-MoE across benchmarks. Our method obtains a stabilizing gain across diverse reasoning tasks, outperforming the other competitor (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-MoE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>) by 6% on ARC-e, 5% on WinoGrande, and 6% on the challenging ARC-c dataset, demonstrating robust generalization capabilities even after substantial compression.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
